Many relational arguments employed in ZisK, such as the permutation argument
of PlonK @EPRINT:GabWilCio19 or the lookup argument of LogUp
@EPRINT:Habock22b, require the prover $cal(P)$ to commit to a set of
polynomials and subsequently demonstrate that these commitments satisfy a
system of algebraic constraints at a specific evaluation point, the
_challenge_. In the interactive setting, the challenge is sampled uniformly at
random and issued by the verifier $cal(V)$. In the non-interactive setting,
the standard Fiat–Shamir heuristic is applied: the challenge is derived by
hashing the committed polynomials along with additional transcript data
required for the soundness of the protocol.

The derivation of challenges becomes substantially more delicate in a
_distributed_ setting, where the proof of an argument is jointly generated by
multiple provers $cal(P)_i$. Specifically, in ZisK, relational arguments are
reduced to univariate sumcheck protocols whose evaluation polynomials must be
consistently instantiated across all provers. Hence, all provers must operate
with an identical challenge value: if provers were to proceed with different
evaluation points, the resulting constraints would be incoherent and
verification would fail trivially. This necessitates a mechanism to derive a
single canonical challenge from the joint set of commitments, and to make it
consistently available to all provers before the protocol can proceed.

More precisely, let ${cal(P)_i}_(i=1)^J$ denote the participating provers.
The process unfolds as follows:
+ Each prover $cal(P)_i$ computes a commitment $c_i in D$ to its local set of
  polynomials, where $D$ denotes the commitment domain.
+ An accumulator party $A$ (assumed honest, see below) iteratively combines
  the commitments via an _accumulator function_ $"acc" : D^k -> D$, which
  compresses blocks of $k$ commitments into a single value, until all $J$
  commitments have been processed.
+ The resulting accumulator value defines the common challenge $alpha$, which
  is broadcast to all provers.

#remark[
  For clarity, we omit throughout this note transcript elements such as the
  public parameters $"pp"$ and auxiliary data, which in practice must also be
  included to guarantee the soundness of the protocol.
]

The accumulator $"acc"$ is required to satisfy:
- *Efficiency*: $"acc"$ should be computable in polynomial time, with cost
  proportional to $k$.
- *Incrementality*: accumulation should support streaming, i.e.,
  intermediate accumulations should depend only on the commitments observed so
  far, without recomputing from scratch.
- *Security*: the derived challenge $alpha$ should be computationally
  indistinguishable from a uniformly random challenge under standard
  assumptions, i.e., it should inherit the pseudorandomness guarantees of
  Fiat–Shamir.

In ZisK, the accumulator is itself embedded inside proof aggregation. Thus,
the aggregation constraints guarantee that $"acc"$ is applied correctly,
allowing us to assume that $A$ is honest. We now turn to the cryptographic
primitive that enables efficient, incremental and secure accumulation:
_multiset hashing_.

= Multiset Hashing

Multiset hashing, introduced by Clarke et al. @AC:CDDGS03 and rooted in the
incremental hashing paradigm of Bellare and Micciancio @EC:BelMic97, provides
exactly the required functionality: it supports incremental computation of a
hash value over dynamically growing multisets. Multiset hashes are incremental
in the sense that, when new members of the multiset become known, the hash can
be updated in time proportional to the new members. They must satisfy a
stronger notion of security than standard hashes, _multiset
collision-resistance_, which says that it must be hard to find two different
multisets that produce the same hash.

Formally, a multiset hash function is a map
$H : cal(M)({0, 1}^*) -> GG$, where $cal(M)(dot)$ denotes multisets and
$(GG, plus.o)$ is a commutative group, satisfying:
- *Multiset Homomorphism*: for any two multisets
  $S, T in cal(M)({0, 1}^*)$, $H(S union T) = H(S) plus.o H(T)$.
- *Multiset Collision-Resistance*: it is computationally infeasible to find
  multisets $S, T in cal(M)({0, 1}^*)$ such that $S != T$ and
  $H(S) = H(T)$.

Bellare and Micciancio introduced for sets, and Clarke et al. later
generalized to multisets, the idea of taking a collision-resistant
hash-to-group function $h : {0, 1}^* -> GG$ and defining the multiset hash
function $H$ over an additive group $(GG, +)$ as
$
  H(S) = m_(s_1) h(s_1) + dots + m_(s_J) h(s_J)
$
for the multiset $S = {s_1^((m_(s_1))), dots, s_J^((m_(s_J)))}$, where
$x^((m_x))$ denotes that the string $x$ appears $m_x$ times in $S$, i.e.,
$m_x$ is the _multiplicity_ of $x$. Multiset homomorphism follows directly
from the definition.

Clarke et al. showed that, if $(GG, +)$ is taken to be addition over $ZZ_p$,
multiset collision-resistance can be proved for a sufficiently large $p$. This
construction, however, requires a secret key, which does not fit our
setting. Moreover, Clarke et al. only considered Wagner's attack
@C:Wagner02 in their security analysis, and refinements of the attack have
appeared since then, e.g., @ANTS:Shallue08, that improve its efficiency.

They also propose to take $(GG, +)$ to be addition over $ZZ_p^n$, the space of
vectors of dimension $n$ with coefficients in $ZZ_p$. In this setting, they
show multiset collision-resistance by a reduction to the short integer
solution (SIS) problem @STOC:Ajtai96, a well-studied lattice problem. This
latter construction, broadly named LtHash, although less efficient than the
previous one, does not require any secret key, and it is the one we focus on
from now on.

= Lattice-Based Multiset Hashing (LtHash)

Fix a positive integer $n$. Following Clarke et al., we instantiate
$(GG, +)$ as the additive group $ZZ_p^n$. Define a base hash
$h : {0, 1}^* -> ZZ_p^n$, obtained, e.g., by iteratively squeezing a standard
cryptographic hash. We define $"LtHash" : cal(M)({0, 1}^*) -> ZZ_p^n$ by
$
  "LtHash"(S) = sum_(i=1)^J m_(s_i) dot h(s_i),
$
where $S = {s_1^((m_(s_1))), dots, s_J^((m_(s_J)))}$, the sum is taken
component-wise over $ZZ_p$ and the product is the scalar multiplication of
$ZZ_p^n$. Again, multiset homomorphism follows directly from the
construction. We now turn to multiset collision-resistance.

== Multiset Collision-Resistance

First, observe that if multiplicities are left unbounded over $ZZ_p$, then
LtHash is not collision-resistant. Indeed, given a multiset $S$, take any
string $y in.not S$ and form $T = S union {y^((p))}$. Then $T != S$ but
$"LtHash"(T) = "LtHash"(S)$. To avoid such trivial collisions, we restrict
our attention to $cal(M)^(< B)({0, 1}^*)$, the family of multisets of
cardinality below $B << p$. This is a stronger requirement than asking that
every element of a multiset have multiplicity much lower than $p$, but it
turns out to be sufficient for ZisK (see the next section).

Collision-resistance of LtHash can be related to the hardness of the SIS
problem, which is believed to remain hard even against quantum adversaries.
For sets, an updated reduction is given in Theorem 2.3 of @EPRINT:LKMW19,
adapting Theorem 6.1 of @EC:BelMic97 to modern models; its adaptation to
multisets, as in Theorem 3 of @AC:CDDGS03, follows directly.

At a high level, the relation goes as follows. For two multisets
$S = {s_1^((m_(s_1))), dots, s_J^((m_(s_J)))}$ and
$T = {t_1^((m_(t_1))), dots, t_K^((m_(t_K)))}$ of
$cal(M)^(< B)({0, 1}^*)$ with $J, K > 0$, let
$U = "supp"(S) union "supp"(T) = {u_1, dots, u_L}$, so that $L > 0$, and
define
$
  A = mat(delim: "[", h(u_1), dots.c, h(u_L)) in ZZ_p^(n times L)
$
and $x in ZZ_p^L$ by $x_i = m_(u_i)^S - m_(u_i)^T$, where $m_u^X$ denotes the
multiplicity of $u$ in the multiset $X$. Then finding a multiset collision
amounts to producing two multisets $S != T$ such that
$
  sum_(i=1)^J m_(s_i) dot h(s_i) - sum_(i=1)^K m_(t_i) dot h(t_i) = bold(0)
  quad <==> quad A x = bold(0).
$
Since both $S$ and $T$ have bounded cardinality and $h$ is a secure hash
function, a collision yields a nontrivial solution $x$ of the SIS instance
with infinity norm below $B$. Consequently, under the hardness of SIS, it is
infeasible to find collisions of LtHash.

== Enforcing Bounded Cardinality

As explained before, the size of every multiset must remain bounded. A simple
and effective technique is to augment each vector with an additional
coordinate fixed to $1$, so that every accumulation tracks the total
multiplicity of its elements, providing a built-in mechanism to enforce the
cardinality bound.

Formally, define the augmented embedding $h'(x) = (h(x), 1) in ZZ_p^(n+1)$.
The LtHash of a multiset $S = {s_1^((m_(s_1))), dots, s_J^((m_(s_J)))}$ is
then
$
  "LtHash"(S) = sum_(i=1)^J m_(s_i) dot h'(s_i)
  = (sum_(i=1)^J m_(s_i) dot h(s_i), sum_(i=1)^J m_(s_i)).
$
The last coordinate records the total multiplicity of the multiset. By
requiring, within proof aggregation, that this coordinate stays strictly below
a fixed bound $B$ (e.g., $2^(20)$), we guarantee that no multiset reaches $B$
elements. This prevents adversaries from building long addition chains or
otherwise bypassing the cardinality bound.

== Concrete Parameters and Security

We use the Lattice Estimator @LATTICE-ESTIMATOR to estimate the security of
LtHash against the best known attacks on SIS. With $n = 372$,
$p = 2^(64) - 2^(32) + 1$ and $B = 2^(20)$,
```sage
from estimator import *

vector_size = 372
goldilocks = 2**64 - 2**32 + 1
bound = 2**20

params = SIS.Parameters(n=vector_size, q=goldilocks, length_bound=bound, norm=oo)
SIS.estimate(params)
```
we obtain
#codly(number-format: none)
```
lattice  :: rop: ≈2^134.3, red: ≈2^133.3, sieve: ≈2^133.3,
            β: 363, η: 373, ζ: 317, d: 1951, prob: 1, ↻: 1, tag: infinity
```
which is sufficient for our $128$-bit security target.

= Application to Distributed Challenge Derivation in ZisK

We now describe how distributed challenge derivation is instantiated in ZisK.
Let $n$ be a positive integer, let $p = 2^(64) - 2^(32) + 1$ be the
Goldilocks prime, and let $h$ be the Poseidon2 hash function
@AFRICACRYPT:GraKhoSch23. ZisK builds on STARKs as its underlying proving
system, where polynomial commitments are the Merkle roots of trees containing
all polynomial evaluations over a predefined domain over $ZZ_p$. Since
Poseidon2 is used in the commitment phase, every commitment $c_i$ lies in the
domain $D = ZZ_p^4$.

For security, the challenge space must be large enough for the target
security level. As ZisK targets $128$-bit security, we fix $ZZ_p^3$ as the
challenge space. Finally, we use the technique of the previous section to
bound the number of accumulated elements by $B$.

The distributed challenge $alpha$ is computed collaboratively by the provers
${cal(P)_i}_(i=1)^J$ as follows:
+ *Commit phase.* Each prover $cal(P)_i$ computes its polynomial commitment
  $c_i in ZZ_p^4$ and sends it to the designated accumulator party $A$.
+ *Initialization and first accumulation.* The accumulator is initialized at
  $"acc"_0 = 0$. Upon receiving the first batch of $k$ commitments
  $C = {c_1, dots, c_k}$, the accumulator builds the multiset
  $S = {s_1^((m_(s_1))), dots, s_ell^((m_(s_ell)))}$ from $C$. For each
  $s_i in S$, it derives a vector embedding by iterated hashing,
  $
    h_(i,1) = h(s_i), quad h_(i,2) = h(h_(i,1)), quad dots, quad
    h_(i,n\/4) = h(h_(i,n\/4-1)),
  $
  where each $h_(i,j) in ZZ_p^4$, and concatenates all intermediate values
  into
  $
    v_i = (h_(i,1), dots, h_(i,n\/4), 1) in ZZ_p^(n+1).
  $
  It then applies LtHash:
  $
    "acc"_1 = "acc"_0 + "acc"(c_1, dots, c_k) = "LtHash"(S) = sum_(i=1)^ell m_(s_i) dot v_i .
  $
+ *Subsequent accumulations.* As further batches of $k$ commitments arrive,
  the accumulator repeats the above process, updating
  $"acc"_i = "acc"_(i-1) + "acc"(dot)$.
+ *Finalization.* After processing all $J$ commitments, i.e., after $J \/ k$
  accumulation rounds, the accumulator outputs the final value
  $"acc" := "acc"_(J\/k)$, which is broadcast to all the provers. Each prover
  continues the protocol using the embedding of $"acc"$ into $ZZ_p^3$ as the
  common challenge $alpha$.

Efficiency, incrementality and security follow directly from the properties
of LtHash. Accumulation is fully parallelizable and can be performed in any
order. Input lengths can also vary, $k' != k$ being permissible, so several
accumulators can be composed hierarchically: two accumulators $A_1$ and $A_2$
may independently process disjoint subsets $C_1$ and $C_2$ of the commitments
$C = {c_1, dots, c_J}$, after which a final aggregation step combines their
outputs.

